Yes — with one important refinement: g is essentially the local readout of a gradient, not a universal constant pointing “down” in some absolute cosmic coordinate system.

In Newtonian form,

\mathbf g=-\nabla\Phi

where \Phi is gravitational potential. So your “compressed gradient” intuition is mathematically close: the vector \mathbf g compresses the local rate and direction of change of gravitational potential into one arrow.

But it is not identical everywhere or in every direction. On Earth, its magnitude and direction vary with altitude, latitude, the planet’s rotation, its nonspherical shape, and local mass distributions. “Down” means approximately toward decreasing effective potential, not universally “south,” “toward the bottom of the page,” or even exactly toward Earth’s geometric center.

And this is where water gets interesting.

Water does not fundamentally obey:

\text{flow toward lower geometric height}

It obeys something closer to:

\text{flow toward lower total potential}

For ordinary groundwater/fluid mechanics, hydraulic head can be written roughly as

H=z+\frac{P}{\rho g}+\frac{v^2}{2g}

with other terms added when needed. So geometric elevation z is only one component.

Therefore water can absolutely:

Flow north. North/south is almost irrelevant to gravity; rivers flow north all over Earth when their potential gradient points that way.

Flow uphill geometrically. Raise the pressure enough and water climbs. That’s what pipes, artesian wells, fountains, siphons under the appropriate conditions, and circulatory systems exploit.

Climb against gravity without a pump. Capillary forces can raise water through tiny pores because surface-energy gradients overcome gravitational potential over that scale.

So if you watch a droplet moving “uphill,” you haven’t defeated the gradient. You’ve probably been looking at the wrong coordinate plane.

That’s a very Pantheon-compatible distinction:

\boxed{\text{apparent uphill on one axis}
\neq
\text{uphill in total potential}}

A ball can move upward in z while still moving downhill through a higher-dimensional energy landscape.

And “space is zero” can be made useful in a related way: an origin is arbitrary. Coordinates tell you where relative to a chosen reference; physics is usually carried by differences, gradients, curvature, and invariants. Zero itself often contains very little information.

So:

0 \rightarrow \Delta \rightarrow \nabla \rightarrow \text{motion}

The zero gives you a reference.
The difference gives you information.
The gradient gives you direction.
The dynamics tell you what actually moves.

Which means your earlier lens idea appears again: “uphill” may be a projection artifact. Rotate into the coordinate system of the governing potential and the supposedly impossible motion becomes downhill again.